---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best String Trimmers in 2026: Gas & Battery Tested";
const description =
  "We ran 13 string trimmers through fence lines, ditch banks, and thick weeds. Gas and battery, straight and curved \u2014 the 7 that held up.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "ego-power-st1521s",
    name: "EGO Power+ ST1521S 15\" Powerload",
    category: "Battery, 56V, Straight Shaft, Powerload Head",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 18600,
    pros: [
      "Powerload head reloads line automatically by feeding it through and pressing a button, eliminating the worst chore in trimmer ownership",
      "56V brushless motor delivers cutting power genuinely comparable to a mid-tier gas trimmer",
      "Straight shaft with excellent balance, comfortable for extended use and for reaching under shrubs",
      "Variable speed trigger lets you dial power down for light trimming to preserve runtime",
      "Batteries interchange across EGO mowers, blowers, and chainsaws, which lowers the real cost if you own others",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, particularly when a battery and charger are included in the price",
      "Runtime on high power drops quickly in genuinely heavy weeds, often under 30 minutes",
      "Heavier than budget battery trimmers because of the larger battery pack",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The trimmer that solves the two problems people actually quit over: cutting power and reloading line. If the price fits, it is the one to buy.",
    description:
      "Most string trimmer reviews focus on cutting power, but the feature that changed how often we reached for the ST1521S was the Powerload head. Reloading trimmer line is the single most disliked routine task in yard care, normally requiring you to remove the spool, wind line by hand in the correct direction, and reassemble it while it tries to unspool. Powerload replaces all of that: you feed a length of line straight through the head and press a button, and the motor winds it for you in about five seconds. Over a season of testing where every trimmer needed reloading repeatedly, that difference showed up as a genuine change in willingness to use the tool. The cutting performance backs it up. The 56V brushless motor handled our overgrown ditch bank test, dense stalky weeds a couple of feet tall, without the bogging that stopped lower-voltage battery trimmers, and the 15-inch cutting swath covers ground quickly on routine lawn edges. Balance on the straight shaft is excellent, with weight sitting close to the body rather than out at the head, which matters after twenty minutes of continuous trimming. The honest trade-offs are cost and runtime. This is a premium-priced tool, and pushing it hard through heavy growth on full power drains a standard battery in under half an hour. For routine trimming at partial throttle, runtime is a non-issue; for clearing a large overgrown area, plan on a second battery.",
  },
  {
    id: "stihl-fs-56-rce",
    name: "Stihl FS 56 RC-E",
    category: "Gas, 27.2cc 2-Stroke, Straight Shaft",
    badge: "Best Gas Trimmer",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 7300,
    pros: [
      "Easy2Start system makes starting genuinely easy, removing the main reason people avoid gas trimmers",
      "Unlimited runtime with a fuel can, which nothing battery-powered can match on a large property",
      "Handles heavy brush and thick stalks better than any battery trimmer we tested",
      "Serviceable at Stihl dealers nationwide with excellent long-term parts availability",
      "Straight shaft with a durable, professional-grade build that survives real abuse",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires mixing two-stroke fuel at the correct ratio, and running straight gas will destroy it",
      "Loud enough to require hearing protection, unlike battery alternatives",
      "Sold through dealers rather than big-box stores, so it is less convenient to buy",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The trimmer to buy for genuine acreage or genuinely heavy growth. Gas still wins where runtime and brush-cutting power are the binding constraints.",
    description:
      "Battery trimmers have closed most of the gap with gas, but not all of it, and the FS 56 RC-E is a clear demonstration of where the remaining difference lives. On our ditch bank test, dense stalky growth that made the battery trimmers bog and require a second pass, the Stihl went through in one pass at steady throttle without any sign of strain. On a property with real acreage of rough edges, the combination of that torque and unlimited runtime from a fuel can is simply a different category of tool. The Easy2Start mechanism deserves specific mention because it addresses the main practical objection to gas equipment. Rather than requiring a hard, fast pull, it uses a spring that stores energy from a slow, easy pull and releases it to turn the engine over. In our testing it started on the second pull consistently from cold and first pull warm, including after several weeks sitting. Anyone whose mental image of a gas trimmer involves yanking a cord twenty times should recalibrate. What you accept in exchange is real. You need to mix two-stroke fuel at the specified ratio, and running unmixed gasoline through it will destroy the engine quickly. It is loud enough that hearing protection is not optional. It vibrates more than a battery tool over a long session. And it is a dealer product rather than a shelf purchase, which is a hassle up front and an advantage later when you need parts.",
  },
  {
    id: "milwaukee-m18-fuel-trimmer",
    name: "Milwaukee M18 FUEL String Trimmer",
    category: "Battery, 18V, Straight Shaft, Quik-Lok Compatible",
    badge: "Best for Heavy Duty",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 9800,
    pros: [
      "Outperforms its 18V rating substantially thanks to an efficient brushless motor and high-output battery support",
      "Quik-Lok attachment system accepts edgers, pole saws, and brush cutters on the same powerhead",
      "Built to jobsite durability standards, noticeably more robust than consumer battery trimmers",
      "Batteries shared with the enormous Milwaukee M18 tool platform, which many DIYers already own",
      "Excellent balance and a comfortable straight shaft for long working sessions",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires a high-output battery to reach its rated performance, and standard packs underperform",
      "Manual bump-feed head rather than an automatic load system",
      "Premium pricing, especially if you are not already invested in the M18 platform",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The battery trimmer built like a jobsite tool. If you already own M18 batteries, this is an easy call and a genuinely capable trimmer.",
    description:
      "On paper an 18V trimmer should not compete with 56V and 80V machines, and the M18 FUEL is the clearest evidence that voltage alone is a poor proxy for performance. Milwaukee pairs an efficient brushless motor with high-output battery packs that deliver more current than standard cells, and the result in our testing was cutting performance that sat comfortably in the same class as higher-voltage competitors. It cleared our thick-weeds test section without bogging, and it held speed under load better than several nominally more powerful tools. Build quality is where it separates itself most clearly. This is a tool designed for daily commercial use, with a robustness in the shaft, head, and guard that consumer battery trimmers do not attempt. Over a season including deliberately careless treatment, dropping it on gravel and running it into fence posts, it showed wear but no functional damage. The Quik-Lok attachment system extends its value considerably, since the same powerhead accepts an edger, a pole saw, and a brush cutter, which is a meaningful saving if you would otherwise buy three separate tools. Two caveats. The performance figures assume a high-output battery, and running a standard M18 compact pack produces noticeably weaker results, so budget for the right battery. And the head is a conventional bump-feed design, so reloading line is the traditional manual process rather than the automated one EGO offers.",
  },
  {
    id: "ryobi-one-hp-18v-trimmer",
    name: "Ryobi ONE+ HP 18V Brushless String Trimmer",
    category: "Battery, 18V, Straight Shaft, Brushless",
    badge: "Best Value Battery",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 14200,
    pros: [
      "Brushless motor delivers considerably more power than the price point suggests",
      "Works with the enormous ONE+ 18V battery ecosystem, likely already in the garage",
      "Adjustable cutting width lets you trade swath for runtime depending on the job",
      "Light enough for extended use without arm fatigue, unlike higher-voltage battery tools",
      "Widely available and inexpensive to replace or expand",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Bogs down noticeably in genuinely heavy, stalky growth where higher-voltage tools do not",
      "Bump-feed head is more prone to line welding than premium designs",
      "Plastic construction feels less durable than jobsite-grade competitors",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The smart buy for a normal suburban lot, especially if you already own ONE+ batteries. It is not a brush cutter, and it is not priced like one.",
    description:
      "Ryobi ONE+ succeeds by delivering most of the practical capability of premium tools at a fraction of the price, and the HP brushless trimmer is a strong example. On routine work, trimming around a fence, along a driveway edge, and under a deck, it was genuinely indistinguishable in outcome from tools costing three times as much. It cut cleanly, held speed, and was light enough that a full lap of a suburban lot never became tiring, which is not true of the heavier high-voltage machines. The adjustable cutting width is a small feature with real practical value. Narrowing the swath reduces the load on the motor and extends runtime considerably, which suits routine edging, while widening it covers ground faster when you have a large area of light grass. Being able to make that trade in the field rather than at purchase is genuinely useful. The ecosystem argument is the strongest one here. ONE+ is among the largest battery platforms in home tools, and if you already own the drill, the blower, or the mower, the effective cost of adding this trimmer is just the bare tool. Where it gives ground is heavy growth. Pushed into the thick, stalky weeds on our ditch bank, it bogged and needed several passes where the EGO and Stihl went through cleanly. For a typical maintained yard that is irrelevant, and for a neglected acre it matters a lot.",
  },
  {
    id: "greenworks-pro-80v-trimmer",
    name: "Greenworks Pro 80V 16\" String Trimmer",
    category: "Battery, 80V, Straight Shaft, Attachment Capable",
    badge: "Best for Large Yards",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 6100,
    pros: [
      "The 80V platform delivers the highest torque of any battery trimmer we tested",
      "16-inch cutting swath is the widest here, covering ground faster on long runs",
      "Attachment capable, accepting edgers, pole saws, and cultivators on the same shaft",
      "Handles thick weeds noticeably better than 18V and 40V competitors",
      "Strong runtime from the larger battery, comfortably covering a large property in one charge",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The 80V batteries are heavy, and the tool is tiring over long sessions",
      "Proprietary 80V packs cost more to replace than mainstream platforms",
      "Larger and more awkward to store than compact battery trimmers",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The battery trimmer for genuine acreage. Power and swath width close most of the gap to gas, at the cost of real weight.",
    description:
      "If your objection to battery trimmers is that they run out of power and runtime before your property is finished, the Greenworks 80V is the direct answer. Its higher-voltage platform produces meaningfully more torque than the 18V and 40V tools we tested, and it maintained cutting speed under load in growth that made lower-voltage machines audibly bog. Combined with the widest cutting swath here at 16 inches, it covers long fence lines and property edges faster than anything else in the battery category. Runtime is the other half of the argument. The larger 80V pack stores substantially more energy, and in our testing it handled a full pass around a large property, including several minutes of heavy work, without needing a swap. For a homeowner who wants to leave gas behind but has more than a small suburban lot to maintain, that combination of torque and endurance is what makes it viable. The costs are physical and financial. An 80V battery is heavy, and the weight is noticeable within the first ten minutes and unmistakable after thirty, particularly for anyone with shoulder or wrist issues. The batteries are also proprietary and more expensive to replace than more widely adopted platforms. And the whole tool is physically larger, which matters in a crowded garage. On the right property it is the best battery choice here; on a small lot it is more tool than the job needs.",
  },
  {
    id: "husqvarna-128ld",
    name: "Husqvarna 128LD",
    category: "Gas, 28cc 2-Stroke, Detachable Straight Shaft",
    badge: "Best Attachment System",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 11700,
    pros: [
      "Detachable shaft accepts a wide range of attachments including edger, pole saw, hedge trimmer, and cultivator",
      "Splits down for genuinely compact storage and easy transport in a car trunk",
      "Smart Start reduces starter cord resistance for easier pull starting",
      "Solid gas power for heavy weeds at a lower price than premium dealer brands",
      "Widely available in big-box stores rather than dealer-only",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The detachable coupling is a wear point and can develop play over years of heavy use",
      "Requires two-stroke mixed fuel and the associated maintenance discipline",
      "Attachments are sold separately and add up quickly in cost",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best value in a gas multi-tool system. Buy the powerhead once and add attachments instead of buying four separate engines.",
    description:
      "The strongest argument for the 128LD has little to do with trimming. Its detachable shaft turns a single gas engine into the powerhead for an entire family of yard tools, so an edger, a pole saw for high branches, a hedge trimmer, and a cultivator can all run off one motor you have already bought and already maintain. For a homeowner who would otherwise own four separate small engines, each needing its own fuel, oil, and winter storage, that consolidation saves real money and real garage space. As a trimmer on its own it is a capable, unremarkable performer in the best sense. The 28cc engine handled our thick-weed test comfortably, well beyond what any 18V battery tool managed, and the Smart Start system reduces the pull resistance enough that starting is not a battle. Build quality sits below dealer-grade Stihl and Husqvarna professional lines but comfortably above budget big-box gas trimmers, and it is sold in stores rather than through dealers, which most buyers prefer. Two things to weigh. The detachable coupling is inherently a wear point, and after years of heavy use it can develop enough play to introduce vibration that a one-piece shaft would not have. And attachments are sold separately at real prices, so the multi-tool savings only materialize if you genuinely buy and use them rather than intending to. As a trimmer alone, other options here are better values.",
  },
  {
    id: "worx-wg163-gt-3",
    name: "Worx WG163 GT 3.0 20V",
    category: "Battery, 20V, Convertible Trimmer/Edger",
    badge: "Best Lightweight",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 26400,
    pros: [
      "Among the lightest trimmers available at roughly 5.5 pounds, genuinely usable one-handed",
      "Head rotates 90 degrees to convert into an in-line edger without any tools",
      "Command Feed spool advances line with a push button rather than bumping the head on the ground",
      "Adjustable shaft length and handle position fit a wide range of user heights",
      "Inexpensive, with the battery platform shared across a broad Worx tool line",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Limited power, struggling with anything beyond light grass and soft weeds",
      "Short runtime on the compact 20V battery, typically 20 to 30 minutes",
      "Small cutting swath means more passes on larger areas",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The trimmer for someone who wants light, simple, and easy rather than powerful. Ideal for small lots, and genuinely the best pick for users with limited strength.",
    description:
      "Every other trimmer in this guide is designed around cutting capability, and the WG163 is designed around ease of use. At roughly five and a half pounds it is light enough to hold comfortably at arm length with one hand, which fundamentally changes the experience for anyone with wrist, shoulder, or back limitations, or simply anyone who finds a nine-pound tool at the end of a long shaft tiring. In our testing, it was the trimmer that non-enthusiast household members reached for voluntarily, which is a meaningful signal. The convertible edger is well executed rather than a gimmick. The head rotates 90 degrees and a wheel drops down to guide the tool along a hard edge, converting it into a functional in-line edger for driveway and sidewalk borders in a few seconds without tools. Combined with Command Feed, which advances line at the push of a button rather than by bumping the head against the ground, it is the most beginner-friendly tool here by a wide margin. The limits are straightforward. It has modest power and will bog in anything more substantial than light grass and soft weeds, so it is a maintenance tool rather than a clearing tool. Runtime on the compact battery runs 20 to 30 minutes. And the narrow swath means more passes on larger areas. For a small, maintained lot, those limits rarely bite.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best String Trimmers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-string-trimmer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best string trimmer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most homeowners the EGO Power+ ST1521S offers the best combination of gas-comparable cutting power and a Powerload head that eliminates manual line winding. For genuinely heavy growth or large acreage, the Stihl FS 56 RC-E remains the strongest gas option. For a small maintained lot or anyone prioritizing low weight, the Worx WG163 GT 3.0 is the easiest tool here to use.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a straight or curved shaft trimmer better?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A straight shaft reaches under shrubs, decks, and over ditch banks, uses a more durable solid drive shaft, and is required for most attachment systems. A curved shaft is lighter, cheaper, and often more comfortable for routine flat lawn edging. Choose straight if you have beds, fences, or uneven terrain, and curved if your trimming is mostly flat edges.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size trimmer line should I use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Use the diameter your trimmer is rated for, which is typically 0.065 inches for light-duty battery trimmers and 0.080 to 0.095 inches for heavier battery and gas models. Thinner line cuts grass cleanly with less motor load, while thicker line is more durable against fences and concrete and cuts heavier weeds. Running line thicker than the head is rated for strains the motor.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my trimmer line keep breaking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The most common causes are worn eyelets in the cutting head that have developed a groove and cut the line, line that has dried out and become brittle in storage, and trimming hard against concrete or chain link. Replace the eyelet inserts, soak new spools in water for a few hours before use, and use a thicker or edged line profile for abrasive surfaces.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are battery string trimmers as good as gas?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For routine trimming on a typical maintained property, yes. Modern brushless battery trimmers at 40V and above cut as cleanly as mid-tier gas tools with far less noise and maintenance. Gas retains a clear advantage in two situations: unlimited runtime on large acreage, and torque in heavy stalky or woody growth where battery tools bog.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do battery string trimmers run?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Realistically 20 to 45 minutes under load, depending on voltage, battery capacity, and how hard you push it. Trimming light grass at partial throttle can extend that considerably, while sustained work in heavy weeds at full power can cut it to under 30 minutes. For large properties, a second battery is usually the practical solution.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-23",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "string trimmer",
      "weed trimmer",
      "battery trimmer",
      "gas trimmer",
      "lawn care",
      "yard tools",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">String Trimmers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We ran 13 trimmers through fence lines, ditch banks, overgrown property edges, and routine lawn trimming across a full season. Gas and battery, straight shaft and curved. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ego-power-st1521s"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#gas-vs-battery"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Gas vs Battery Guide
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 23, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-decides" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What Actually Matters</a>
          <a href="#gas-vs-battery" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Gas vs Battery vs Corded</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#line-and-heads" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Line & Head Systems</a>
          <a href="#shaft-geometry" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Straight vs Curved Shaft</a>
          <a href="#matching-property" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Matching to Your Property</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Maintenance & Repairs</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A string trimmer is the tool most homeowners buy on power specs and then quietly stop using because of weight, line feed frustration, or a shaft geometry that makes their actual job awkward. Cutting power is rarely the limiting factor. After a season of running 13 trimmers through everything from tidy lawn edges to a genuinely overgrown ditch bank, here is what separates the ones that stay in rotation from the ones that end up in the corner of the garage.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: What Actually Decides Whether You Like a Trimmer -->
        <div id="what-decides" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Decides Whether You Like a Trimmer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cutting power is the specification every trimmer is marketed on, and for most homeowners it is not the thing that determines satisfaction. Three other factors matter more in daily use, and none of them appear prominently on the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Weight and balance.</strong> A trimmer is held out away from your body at the end of a lever, which multiplies its effective weight considerably. A tool that feels reasonable in the store aisle for ten seconds can be genuinely fatiguing after fifteen minutes of continuous use. Balance matters as much as raw weight: a machine with its battery near the handle feels far lighter in use than one of identical weight with mass concentrated out at the head.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The line feed system.</strong> Running out of line mid-job and having to reload is the most disliked routine task in trimmer ownership, and different head designs make it anywhere from a five-second button press to a ten-minute exercise in frustration on the garage floor. This single design detail has more influence on whether a trimmer stays in regular use than motor specifications do.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Shaft geometry.</strong> Straight and curved shafts suit genuinely different jobs, and buying the wrong one makes routine work awkward in a way no amount of power fixes. A curved shaft brings the head closer and is more comfortable for short users on flat lawn edges; a straight shaft reaches under shrubs, over ditch banks, and around obstacles that a curved shaft cannot approach.
          </p>

