          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Gas vs Battery vs Corded
          </h2>

          <p>
            The power source decision sets the ceiling on what the tool can cut and the floor on how much maintenance it demands. For most suburban properties the answer has shifted decisively in the last several product generations.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery</h3>

          <p>
            Modern brushless battery trimmers handle everything a typical maintained yard presents: lawn edges, fence lines, around trees and mailboxes, and moderate weeds. They start instantly, weigh less than comparable gas tools, require essentially no maintenance beyond line replacement, and are quiet enough to use early on a Saturday without irritating neighbors. The constraints are runtime, typically 20 to 45 minutes under real load, and reduced torque in genuinely heavy, woody growth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas</h3>

          <p>
            Gas remains the answer for two situations: large properties where runtime is the binding constraint, and heavy, stalky, or woody growth where torque matters more than convenience. A gas trimmer with a fuel can attached to your belt runs all day. The costs are two-stroke fuel mixing, more vibration, considerably more noise, pull starting, and seasonal maintenance including spark plugs, air filters, and fuel stabilization.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded Electric</h3>

          <p>
            Corded trimmers are the cheapest and lightest option and never run out of power, but the extension cord is a genuine practical limitation. Managing a cord around trees, beds, and fences while operating a spinning cutting head is awkward, and the practical reach limit of about 100 feet from an outlet rules out most of the property for most homes. They make sense for very small lots and for occasional use.
          </p>

