        <!-- SECTION 4: Line Diameter, Shape, and Feed Systems -->
        <div id="line-and-heads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Line Diameter, Shape, and Feed Systems
          </h2>

          <p>
            The consumable and the head that holds it have more day-to-day effect on your experience than almost anything else about the tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Line Diameter</h3>

          <p>
            Thinner line, around 0.065 inches, cuts light grass cleanly with less motor load and better runtime on battery tools. Thicker line, 0.080 to 0.095 inches, is far more durable against fences and concrete and cuts heavier weeds, at the cost of more power draw and more vibration. Match the diameter to your tool's rating: running line thicker than the head is designed for strains the motor and can damage the tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Line Shape</h3>

          <p>
            Round line is the cheapest, most durable, and least aggressive, and it is the right default for routine trimming. Twisted line reduces the noise and vibration that round line produces. Square, star, and serrated profiles have cutting edges that slice through thick weeds far more effectively, at the cost of breaking more readily against hard surfaces. For a maintained lawn use round; for heavy growth, an edged profile makes a real difference.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bump Feed Heads</h3>

          <p>
            The most common design. Tapping the spinning head against the ground releases a measured length of line. Simple, reliable, and universally understood, but reloading means disassembling the spool and winding line by hand in the correct direction, which is where most trimmer frustration comes from.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Automatic and Powered Feed Heads</h3>

          <p>
            Automatic heads advance line by sensing when it has shortened, and powered load systems like EGO Powerload wind a fed-through length of line at the press of a button. The latter genuinely eliminates the worst chore in trimmer ownership and is worth paying for if you trim frequently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fixed-Line Heads</h3>

          <p>
            These accept pre-cut lengths of line that you insert individually, with no spool at all. Reloading takes seconds and there is nothing to tangle or weld, but you replace individual pieces more often. They are popular with people who have given up on spools entirely, and replacement heads are available for many trimmers.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Why Your Line Keeps Welding Itself</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              When line runs out and the head keeps spinning hot, or when a trimmer is stored in a hot garage, the nylon layers on the spool can fuse together, which is why line stops feeding and the spool has to be pulled apart. Two habits prevent almost all of it: stop trimming as soon as the line gets short rather than running it out, and soak new spools in water for a few hours before installing, since hydrated nylon is more flexible and resists welding.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Straight Shaft vs Curved Shaft -->
        <div id="shaft-geometry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Straight Shaft vs Curved Shaft
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is a genuine functional difference rather than a style preference, and it is the spec most likely to make a buyer quietly dissatisfied without being able to say why.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Straight Shaft</h3>

          <p>
            A straight shaft puts the cutting head farther from you and in line with the shaft, which lets it reach under shrubs, beneath deck edges, and out over the lip of a ditch or retaining wall where a curved shaft cannot go. It generally uses a solid drive shaft rather than a flexible cable, which transmits power more efficiently and lasts longer under load. It is also the geometry accepted by attachment systems. Taller users typically find it more comfortable because it does not require stooping.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Curved Shaft</h3>

          <p>
            A curved shaft brings the cutting head closer to the operator, which many people find more natural and better balanced for routine work along flat lawn edges. It tends to be lighter and cheaper. The limitations are reach and drive: it cannot get under low obstacles as effectively, and it typically uses a flexible drive cable that is less efficient and wears faster than a solid shaft.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which to Buy</h3>

          <p>
            If your trimming is mostly flat lawn edges, sidewalks, and around a mailbox, a curved shaft is comfortable and sufficient. If you have shrub beds, fences, drainage ditches, retaining walls, or any interest in attachments, buy a straight shaft. Most of the trimmers in this guide are straight-shaft designs precisely because that flexibility suits more properties.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Matching the Trimmer to Your Property -->
        <div id="matching-property" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Matching the Trimmer to Your Property
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small Suburban Lot, Maintained</h3>

          <p>
            A light 18V or 20V battery trimmer is genuinely sufficient and the weight advantage is worth more than extra power you will never use. Prioritize a good feed system and low weight over voltage. The Worx and Ryobi options here are aimed squarely at this case.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Half Acre to an Acre, Mixed Conditions</h3>

          <p>
            Step up to a 40V to 56V straight-shaft battery trimmer with a wider swath. You want enough torque for occasional heavy patches and enough runtime to finish in one session, and this is where the EGO sits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acreage, Fence Lines, and Rough Edges</h3>

          <p>
            Either gas or a high-voltage battery platform with a spare pack. Runtime becomes the binding constraint before cutting power does, and a fuel can never needs recharging. Consider a brush cutter attachment or a dedicated brush blade for anything woody.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Anyone With Strength or Mobility Limitations</h3>

          <p>
            Weight is the single most important specification, ahead of everything else. A five and a half pound trimmer that you use comfortably beats a nine pound trimmer that sits unused. Look for adjustable shaft length, a rotating handle, and a push-button line feed rather than a bump head that requires tapping the tool against the ground.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Maintenance and Common Trimmer Problems -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Maintenance and Common Trimmer Problems
          </h2>

          <p>
            String trimmers are simple machines, and nearly all of their failures come from a handful of predictable causes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Line will not feed.</strong> Almost always welded line on the spool from heat, or line wound in the wrong direction or crossed over itself during reloading. Pull the spool, unwind it, and rewind in the direction of the arrow marked on the spool, keeping the layers neat and side by side rather than overlapping.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gas trimmer will not start after storage.</strong> Ethanol-blended fuel degrades within a few weeks and gums up small carburetors, which is the leading cause of small-engine failure. Either drain the fuel before storage or use a stabilizer, and consider ethanol-free fuel for equipment used seasonally. A fresh spark plug and a clean air filter resolve most of the rest.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery runtime dropping over time.</strong> Lithium packs degrade with cycles and with heat. Storing batteries at a partial charge in a temperate location rather than fully charged in a hot garage measurably extends their usable life. Expect several seasons of good service from a quality pack.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Excessive vibration.</strong> On gas trimmers, check for a worn flexible drive cable on curved-shaft models or a loose shaft coupling on detachable systems. On any trimmer, an unbalanced head from unevenly extended line causes vibration that disappears once both sides are trimmed to equal length.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cutting head damage.</strong> Trimming against concrete, brick, and chain link wears line and eyelets rapidly. Replaceable eyelet inserts are inexpensive on most heads, and swapping them out when they get grooved prevents the groove from cutting line prematurely, which is a common cause of what feels like a line that keeps breaking for no reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best string trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners the EGO Power+ ST1521S offers the best combination of gas-comparable cutting power and a Powerload head that eliminates manual line winding. For genuinely heavy growth or large acreage, the Stihl FS 56 RC-E remains the strongest gas option. For a small maintained lot or anyone prioritizing low weight, the Worx WG163 GT 3.0 is the easiest tool here to use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Straight or curved shaft?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A straight shaft reaches under shrubs, decks, and over ditch banks, uses a more durable solid drive shaft, and is required for most attachment systems. A curved shaft is lighter, cheaper, and often more comfortable for routine flat lawn edging. Choose straight if you have beds, fences, or uneven terrain, and curved if your trimming is mostly flat edges.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size trimmer line should I use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the diameter your trimmer is rated for, which is typically 0.065 inches for light-duty battery trimmers and 0.080 to 0.095 inches for heavier battery and gas models. Thinner line cuts grass cleanly with less motor load, while thicker line is more durable against fences and concrete and cuts heavier weeds. Running line thicker than the head is rated for strains the motor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my trimmer line keep breaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most common causes are worn eyelets in the cutting head that have developed a groove and cut the line, line that has dried out and become brittle in storage, and trimming hard against concrete or chain link. Replace the eyelet inserts, soak new spools in water for a few hours before use, and use a thicker or edged line profile for abrasive surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are battery trimmers as good as gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For routine trimming on a typical maintained property, yes. Modern brushless battery trimmers at 40V and above cut as cleanly as mid-tier gas tools with far less noise and maintenance. Gas retains a clear advantage in two situations: unlimited runtime on large acreage, and torque in heavy stalky or woody growth where battery tools bog.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a battery trimmer run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Realistically 20 to 45 minutes under load, depending on voltage, battery capacity, and how hard you push it. Trimming light grass at partial throttle can extend that considerably, while sustained work in heavy weeds at full power can cut it to under 30 minutes. For large properties, a second battery is usually the practical solution.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common String Trimmer Buying Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Trimmer regret is remarkably consistent, and it almost never comes down to buying an underpowered tool.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for Peak Power Instead of Weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy high-voltage trimmer that is genuinely tiring after fifteen minutes gets used less than a lighter tool with less power. Unless you regularly cut heavy stalky growth, the extra torque is capability you will rarely use, paid for with fatigue you will feel every single time. Pick up the tool and hold it out at arm length before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Line Feed System</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The head design determines whether reloading takes five seconds or fifteen frustrating minutes on the garage floor, and it is the single biggest predictor of whether a trimmer stays in regular use. Powered load systems and fixed-line heads are worth real money over a conventional bump-feed spool if you trim often.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Line Thicker Than the Tool Is Rated For</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thicker line seems like a free upgrade and is not. It increases motor load, drains battery faster, increases vibration, and on smaller tools can cause overheating and premature motor failure. Check the rated diameter in the manual and stay within it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing a Gas Trimmer With Untreated Fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ethanol-blended gasoline degrades in weeks and gums up small carburetors, which is the single most common reason a gas trimmer will not start in spring. Either run the tank dry before storage or add a fuel stabilizer, and consider ethanol-free fuel for seasonal equipment. This one habit prevents most off-season small-engine repair bills.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

